		},
		{
			"id":"027126bde7618de0",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Break\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9210,
			"y":1170,
			"width":100,
			"height":176
		},
		{
			"id":"107dd7e99e789cc7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Applications to Real Life",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9480,
			"y":685,
			"width":260,
			"height":70
		},
		{
			"id":"ad196ad24e2d469f",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Classical Scaling \nWhere it fails with computational chemistry",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9361,
			"y":1160,
			"width":449,
			"height":192
		},
		{
			"id":"244ef837ab41a08c",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"## Quantum Computing\n- Origin\n- Qubits and Gates\n- Algorithms as Circuits\n- Complexity and Mapping",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":9870,
			"y":1170,
			"width":450,
			"height":192
		},
		{
			"id":"c20c9f3c2a98ae1e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Recap",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8480,
			"y":2462,
			"width":260,
			"height":140
		},
		{
			"id":"2326ac3b09a27d77",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Atomic ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8800,
			"y":1880,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{
			"id":"335dded305b5b466",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Recap",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8480,
			"y":1962,
			"width":260,
			"height":140
		},
		{"id":"5fd207cae5d93e63","type":"text","text":"# REMD (Replica Exchange MD) \nimproves sampling by running many parallel simulations at different temperatures and doing Monte Carlo swaps between neighboring replicas. Prevents low-temperature runs from getting trapped in local minima. But you need tens of replicas even for a 20-residue protein — you multiply compute cost by replica count, partially canceling the benefit.","x":-1680,"y":1620,"width":566,"height":240},
		{"id":"70468e39e84eb382","type":"text","text":"# MSA Depth Problem\ndata dependency of deep learning methods.\n\nAlphaFold2's Evoformer transformer learns **statistical couplings** between residue positions from the MSA. No MSA = no couplings = the model is essentially guessing based only on local sequence context, with no long-range structural constraints. The whole coevolutionary signal that makes AlphaFold2 powerful simply doesn't exist for orphan proteins.","x":-120,"y":1520,"width":566,"height":280},
		{"id":"d6aa944825a4447b","type":"text","text":"# Mutations\n\nThe paper demonstrates this with **Trp-cage variants TC10b and TC5b** — 85% sequence identity, 3 point mutations, NMR structures with only 0.8 Å RMSD between them (nearly identical experimentally). But compute their free energy landscapes via MD at their respective melting points:\n\n- TC5b: single clean global minimum at -13.5 kJ/mol, textbook folding funnel\n- TC10b: **two distinct folding pathways**, two separate minima at -9.01 and -9.4 kJ/mol, rugged landscape\n\nSame experimental structure, completely different energy landscape. AlphaFold2 predicts structure from the MSA signal, which is similar for both. But the **dynamic behavior and folding thermodynamics** are different — which matters for drug binding, because drugs interact with conformational ensembles, not static structures. The confidence scores (pLDDT) for mutated residues are known to be unreliable.","x":-120,"y":1020,"width":566,"height":471},
		{"id":"a62987e8dda7c50f","type":"text","text":"# Non-MD ab initio methods (PEP-FOLD3, Quark, Rosetta fragment assembly):\n\n- Hard wall at ~20-50 residues\n- Run times acceptable below this (<14 hours reported)\n- Above this: simply intractable due to search space explosion","x":-1680,"y":1040,"width":566,"height":220},
		{"id":"5deac9a30a391246","type":"text","text":"# All-atom MD (D.E. Shaw's Anton supercomputer, the gold standard)\n\nCan fold proteins accurately, though TC10b (20 residues) required 208 µs of simulation time\nThe authors' own hardware (16 cores + 4 A100 GPUs) generates 725 ns per 24h wall time for a 9092-atom system\nAt that rate, reproducing the Shaw result takes 287 days for a 20-residue peptide\nThat's 10 months for the smallest meaningful benchmark. For 80-residue proteins it becomes completely impractical.","x":-1680,"y":1300,"width":566,"height":284},
		{"id":"55fe6b5fa878befc","type":"text","text":"# Ruggedness\nRuggedness of the energy landscape is the key indicator of quantum advantage. More rugged = more local minima = more uncertainty about the search space = larger quantum speedup. This is the \"worst case\" in algorithmic complexity analysis, which is exactly where quantum shines. Quantum tunneling lets the algorithm cross energy barriers probabilistically with no classical analog.","x":-1680,"y":348,"width":568,"height":265},
		{"id":"1432ffb58fc026e5","type":"text","text":"# Classical Ab Initio Methods\n\nFigure 3 in the paper shows this concretely — at 100 residues you're looking at 10^95 conformations","x":-1680,"y":810,"width":566,"height":180},
		{"id":"40ab7bfa0092079b","type":"text","text":"> classical ab initio methods have a hard complexity wall around 50 residues for practical use. ","x":-1020,"y":847,"width":440,"height":107},
		{"id":"bee42f78b0c285bb","type":"text","text":"# AlphaFold2 and Template-Based Methods\n\nAlphaFold2 and RoseTTaFold can handle proteins up to ~2700 residues. Impressive. ","x":-540,"y":806,"width":566,"height":148},
		{
			"id":"8221ed71f08994ba",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"# Microscopic ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":8800,
			"y":2820,
			"width":260,
			"height":60
		},
		{"id":"1ef4d29e0c8c0854","type":"text","text":"TC10b Miniprotein (Trp-Cage Variant)In computational biology and protein folding research, TC10b is a highly studied, artificially engineered variant of the Trp-cage miniprotein.\n\n It is one of the smallest known functional proteins. TC10b was designed to have a more stable alpha-helical structure compared to the original Trp-cage variant (TC5b).\n\nBecause of its rapid folding speed and high thermal stability, researchers frequently use it to model how proteins fold and unfold in computer simulations and experiments.","x":-2460,"y":1300,"width":621,"height":284},
		{"id":"b258864c784b3081","type":"file","file":"media-attachments/Pasted image 20260602090902.png","x":-2614,"y":2260,"width":841,"height":252},
		{"id":"20bf114d66e21e26","type":"text","text":"1. Coarse-grain conformation search.\n2. Convert output to PDB format.\n3. Reconstruct all-atom structure.\n4. Refinement","x":-1680,"y":2315,"width":507,"height":143},
		{"id":"6d6a8611e4a59608","type":"text","text":"# ML-accelerated MD (neural network potentials)\nTrain neural nets on all-atom trajectories to replace the force field evaluation. Faster per step, but requires long all-atom runs to generate training data first. Not generalizable — can't extrapolate to sequences far from the training set. One promising approach (ML x MELD x MD) uses ML-derived residue-residue contacts as spatial restraints, successfully predicted up to 200-residue structures, but still requires the classical training infrastructure","x":-1680,"y":1900,"width":566,"height":280},
		{"id":"04bf5c1f18fea963","type":"text","text":"# Coarse-grain conformation search\n1. Represent protein as a chain on a tetrahedral lattice (4 possible turn directions per bond).\n2. Score conformations using Miyazawa-Jernigan (MJ) potentials — a 20×20 empirical matrix of pairwise amino acid interaction energies, derived from contact frequencies in known PDB structures. This is your classical prior injected into the quantum cost function.\n3. Formulate as an Ising Hamiltonian: binary variables encode turn sequences, quadratic and higher-order terms encode MJ interaction energies for residue pairs brought into contact by each conformation.\n4. Run VQE with RealAmplitudes ansatz on IBM_Cleveland (real hardware, not simulation)","x":-1060,"y":2176,"width":506,"height":420},
		{"id":"262d16e4a10ab3ed","type":"file","file":"media-attachments/Pasted image 20260602091218.png","x":-420,"y":2207,"width":710,"height":359},
		{"id":"651dc789f7f0b8b8","type":"text","text":"VQE beats brute force on the same Hamiltonian. The exhaustive search found RMSD 1.88 Å, VQE found 1.78 Å. This seems paradoxical — brute force finds the true ground state, VQE is heuristic. The explanation: there are multiple local minima close in energy (Figure 4a pattern). The true energy minimum isn't necessarily the structure closest to the experimental crystal structure. VQE's heuristic sampling of near-optimal conformations happened to land on one that's geometrically more accurate, even though it's not the energy minimum. This is actually a feature, not a bug — the free energy landscape has multiple minima and the crystal structure corresponds to one of them, not necessarily the global energy minimum.","x":446,"y":2241,"width":710,"height":290},
		{"id":"d8f095e6c4567f25","type":"text","text":"# Optimization Problem: \nSet up a cost Hamiltonian H_cost whose ground state encodes the minimum-energy conformation. QAOA and VQE are your solvers. At low circuit depth QAOA doesn't beat classical. At high depth it increasingly approximates Grover and gets fractional speedup.","x":-2366,"y":480,"width":566,"height":240},
		{"id":"de2804ee3a1629c7","type":"file","file":"media-attachments/Pasted image 20260602083252.png","x":-2582,"y":-100,"width":782,"height":230},
		{"id":"6ec17e1bfe742169","type":"text","text":"# Protein folding","x":-2585,"y":140,"width":265,"height":60},
		{"id":"8c538aa7d7562042","type":"text","text":"# Search problem\nThe database is all possible conformers. The marked item is the lowest-energy one. Grover gives you O(√N) vs O(N) classical. Optimality is proven — Grover defines a geodesic in quantum Hilbert space, so any other quantum search algorithm is just a parametrized approximation of it.","x":-2366,"y":240,"width":566,"height":220},
		{"id":"f668bf8055935915","type":"file","file":"media-attachments/Pasted image 20260602085650.png","x":-3180,"y":720,"width":682,"height":360},
		{"id":"2dda5195d537a7a6","type":"text","text":"# Discretized Search Space\nThe conformational search space is 3^(2(n-1)) for a protein of length n (each of n-1 peptide bonds has two flanking dihedral angles φ and ψ, each discretized to 3 states).","x":-2366,"y":810,"width":566,"height":180},
